    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In regions of crisis and conflict, communities of refugees and internally displaced persons (IDP) may face disruptions in accessing learning opportunities. Throughout history, educational systems have been impacted by violence, natural disasters, and other emergency situations. At the same time, humanitarian and governmental efforts have overcome complex logistical challenges to deliver survival supplies to affected areas. The crates that carry food, fuel and other critical supplies can also serve as a platform for bringing interactive educational materials, and the hope is that learning and skill development opportunities can have lasting impacts beyond the duration of the temporary supply deliveries. This project proposes to harness existing distribution channels that reach remote refugee and IDP communities to provide tools and resources for learning. Using a process of co-design, the project team is working with collaborating communities and organizations located around the initial region of focus in Northern Uganda. Through creative re-use of materials like cardboard boxes, Ugandan innovators are turning packaging from short-term deliveries into a platform for longer-term skill building.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Huang, Jessica. (2015). Beyond Boxes: Integrating learning opportunities into supply deliveries and rethinking relief. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/cd230bz8442",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "displayLabel": "Preferred Citation",
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
